= System boundary =

== Overview ==
A '''system boundary''' is the distinction used to separate a system of interest from its environment. The boundary determines what is treated as inside the system for a particular analysis and what is treated as external context.

== Definition ==
In systems practice, a boundary is not merely a physical edge. It is an analytical distinction. SEBoK summarizes Checkland's formulation as a distinction made by an observer between an entity treated as a system and its environment.

== Scope & importance ==
Changing the boundary can change the problem. Components, stakeholders, inputs, outputs, constraints, and feedback that appear external under one framing may become internal under another. Boundary choice therefore affects explanation, responsibility, measurement, and design.

== Core concepts ==
Useful boundary questions include: What is inside? What is outside? What crosses the boundary? Which observer or purpose defines it? Is the boundary physical, organizational, informational, temporal, or conceptual?

== Examples ==
A cell membrane marks a biological boundary while still permitting selective exchange. In a software service, the chosen system boundary may include only the application or may also include databases, operators, cloud infrastructure, and external APIs.

== Applications / implications ==
System boundaries are used in systems analysis, engineering, ecology, organizations, software architecture, risk analysis, and any model that must state what it includes and excludes.

== Epistemic notes ==
Boundaries are often purpose-dependent. Treating a boundary as objectively given when it was selected for an analysis can hide assumptions.

